{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pxk7iabHm82nHcuA1R5HYK","type":"tool_result","content":"# Day 7 — Node by Node (script-analysis, node operation graphs)\n\nConcept: one evidence.zip with 61 members (PowerShell samples, JScript/Batch/VBScript/Python/Bash/Node operation\ngraphs); each graph decodes to a plan (steps/adapter/campaign/indicator) and is sorted into a role.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes. TT attempt 26b7da83 failed because its download was the GM body `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` (24 B saved as evidence.zip, sha != contract sha256), so every field came out None/default. The harvested variants 49b73141 + 7d6f8d53 were already answered 469/469 by the current solver (spot-checked against the raw bytes; their expected.json is solver-derived, not GM-verified). Parse rule: if every artifact is a small `{\"error\": ...}` JSON, exit non-zero (solve.py now does) instead of guessing roles. The bytes-less case was removed from cases/ (still kept under time_trial/attempts/26b7da83).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none (Day 8 TT). The start returned 503 `training_rotation_invalid` with no attempt and no artifact, so there were no variant bytes. The solver answers the TT contract's 615 ids 615/615 on the single's bytes. Rule: a start error is an orchestrator retry, not a solver change.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509). Same 5-set bundle shape (outer zip holding evidence-01..05.zip) as the GM-verified auto-mastery cases; the solver answered 3075/3075 but the attempt EXPIRED (submit RemoteDisconnected after 5.42 s solve on Homebase). expected.json = solver output, spot-checked against the raw bytes (PS base64 record, VBS Codec_ field, While/if-errorlevel control). Speed fix: sets solve in forked processes (group_by_set + ProcessPoolExecutor, sequential fallback); the nested-zip index no longer decrypts every script leaf just to sniff for PK (375 PBKDF2s -> only the members asked, per set, in parallel); powerplay.xor is one big-int XOR.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D23). It is permanently UNVERIFIABLE: the D8 attempt EXPIRED (no GM grade), so expected.json stays {}. The D23 solver's 3075 answers equal the D8 solver's submission (mastery/attempts/7432c509/last_timed.json) 3075/3075, and that solver's other D8 Mastery runs were GM-verified. The real failure was SPEED: ~1100 serial AES PBKDF2s on the D23 Mastery bundle. Rule: the WinZip-AES salt sits right after each local header, so derive every member key up front in a thread pool (OpenSSL PBKDF2 releases the GIL) and look it up in the pyzipper PBKDF2 patch (solve.py `_prefetch_keys`/`_KEYS`). Profiled time dropped from 9.8 s to 5.1 s on auto-mastery-e24c070d; answers unchanged.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D24 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075, the same answers as before this change). The real failure was SPEED on the single (2.61 s under load against a 2.5 s budget). Rule: a one-set contract now splits its questions BY ARTIFACT across forked workers (k = min(#artifacts, CPUs in the affinity mask, 4); same ProcessPoolExecutor path as Mastery sets, sequential fallback, output re-sorted into contract order), and the node JScript decode runs in a thread that overlaps the Python analysis (joined only when the first JS member is analysed). Single: 1.4 s -> 0.6 s (12 CPU), 1.0 s with `taskset -c 0,1`; answers identical to the sequential run (1516/1516 GM-verified).\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D25 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075 in 0.66 s, single 1571/1571 GM-verified in 0.38 s, 0.4-0.5 s pinned to 2 CPUs). The flagged 2.59 s single / in-window timeout was measured under host load (loadavg ~9 with parallel generalize workers): sequential cProfile is 0.73 s, of which ~0.2 s is 341 module-level `re.compile`s in `lib.codec` + 88 in `lib.warmups_codecs` and ~0.25 s `lib.codec.peel_plan` for the bash members (already the fast path vs the ~60 ms/file interpreter). No solver change; the remaining wins are lib-side (below).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D26 speed re-flag: single 5.61 s under host load, 1.2 s idle). Hot path was `_codec_hint` -> `lib.codec.find_codecs` (~27 ms/file, peel_plan + `_cs_fold_codecs` fallbacks) on every bash/VBS member. Rule: decode the plain `Codec_<id> = \"rep|transform|key\"` + `Material_<id>` pair directly first (`codec._CODEC_RE` + `material_text` + `decode`, skipped for `#@~^` encoded blocks); the full find_codecs scan runs only when that finds no plan. Hint is identical to find_codecs on every bash/VBS member across all cases; single 1.2 s -> 0.7 s, answers unchanged.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- `lib.codec` / `lib.warmups_codecs` (D25 re-measure): 341 + 88 regexes compiled at import = ~0.2 s of a 0.73 s sequential single. Compile lazily (e.g. a `functools.cache`d `_re(name)` accessor) -- it is now the biggest fixed cost of every script-lane solver.\n- `lib.codec` import is ~0.3 s, almost all of it from ~340 module-level `re.compile`s (cProfile, D24 single). Compiling lazily would save that on every script-lane solver start.\n- Solver skeleton: when `group_by_set` gives one group, split the questions by artifact across forked workers (independent members), like D24 solve.py `main()`.\n- `lib.common.gm_error_body(artdir) -> bytes|None`: detect a GM error JSON (`rate_limited`, ...) saved in place of an\n  artifact, so every solver fails loudly. Better still: the harness download step (hctf/hb_timed.py) should retry on\n  `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` and never save it as the artifact. That would have saved this timed attempt.\n- `lib.common.iter_entries(root, leaf_ext=...)`: don't load a member just to sniff for a nested zip when its name has a script/text extension (.js/.ps1/.py/.vbs/.cmd/.bat/.sh/.mjs/.php/...). Today it decrypts every AES member of a Mastery bundle up front (375 PBKDF2 = ~0.2 s locally, many times that on Homebase), and each forked set repeats it. See `_iter_entries` in this solve.py.\n- `lib.common._zip_open`: prefetch WinZip-AES keys for all members in parallel (salt = bytes after the local header; `hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac` in a ThreadPoolExecutor), as D23 solve.py `_prefetch_keys` does. Every script-lane Mastery solver pays ~1 ms x members serially today.\n- `lib.codec`: ~295 module-level `re.compile`s cost ~0.3 s per solver start, and `_find_codecs_uncached` runs ~16 regex probes per text (4669 searches / 287 texts = 0.4 s on the D23 Mastery). Compile lazily and gate each detector on a cheap substring test (`'Codec_' in text`, ...).\n- `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` + `material_text` + `decode` reading first and return early on a plan, before the carrier/C#-fold/peel fallbacks (each script-lane solver pays ~27 ms per member for them otherwise). Also: `peel_plan`'s wall-clock `budget` makes results depend on host load.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D27 speed cycle): the single took 2-5.8 s because `analyse_node` (.mjs/.cjs members, 8 per single) ran the full `codec.find_codecs` peel scan (~170 ms/file, `_awksed_codecs`/`peel_plan`). Parse rule: decode the declared `Codec_<id>` + `Material_<id>` pair directly first (same fast path as `_codec_hint`) and fall back to `find_codecs` only when that yields no `steps=` plan -> single 0.7 s, all answers unchanged (52/52 cases pass; 7432c509 still unverifiable, 3075/3075 filled).\n\n## Proposed lib additions (D27 speed)\n- `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` spec + `material_text` + `decode` path before `_awksed_codecs` (the peel scan dominates script-lane solver time whenever a solver calls find_codecs unconditionally).\n----\n1:# Triage: script-analysis-day-28-terraform-cloud-timeline\n3:**Prompt:** Terraform HCL describes cloud resources, expressions, and dependencies that change over time. Assemble the timeline from resources, prior scripts, and relationship evidence — without planning or applying anything — and find the change that happened out of order.\n15:- `jscript-benign (host-context.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, host, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n16:- `jscript-stager (memory-stage.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, url, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n17:- `jscript-dropper (embedded-write.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, path, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n18:- `jscript-downloader (remote-write.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, url, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n19:- `jscript-launcher (local-dispatch.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, path, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n20:- `jscript-chain (staged-handoff.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, next-stage, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify the decoded ordered behavior graph by its complete terminal intent. Use benign_utility for decode_con\n21:- `jscript-uploader (outbound-transfer.js)` | role, attack, adapter, campaign, url, function-style, invocation-style, property-style | 8 open | enum/exact_text/pattern | Classify t